# openOODA/INDEX — The Polyrepo

File: openOODA/docs/INDEX.oot

This file is the overview of the openOODA project as a whole. It is the
front-door copy for human visitors; the agent-facing front door is at
`openOODA/ANCHOR.oot`.

## 1. What it is

The openOODA polyrepo. Nine independent repositories that together form
a sovereign, capability-secure systems language for the AI era. One
`.oo` source compiles to four native targets. Every effect requires an
explicit capability token. The standard library has zero ambient
authority. Each repo owns its own surface, version, and release; the
goal is to make them as independent of each other as possible while
remaining functionally aligned.

The repositories cooperate through a single `ooda` binary and a content-
addressed package registry. The compiler is the substrate; the standard
library, package manager, CLI driver, language server, MCP server, and
runtime are siblings, not dependencies.

## 2. Surface

The nine polyrepo repos (as of 2026-09-03):

| Repo        | Role                                                   | Status      |
|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| `openOODA/` | Governance: rules, north star, RFCs, polyrepo topology | v1.0.8      |
| `ooda/`     | Legacy monorepo (cleanup gated on oodac; see §5)       | deprecated |
| `std/`      | Standard library: 8 functional domains, 173 modules     | v1.0.3      |
| `opm/`      | Package manager: CAS-by-sha, capability-tagged         | v0.8.x      |
| `ooda/`     | The `ooda` binary driver, 13 subcommands                | v0.2.1      |
| `lsp/`      | Language server: 16 methods, 5 editor integrations     | v0.6.3      |
| `mcp/`      | MCP server: 23 tools, 5 tiers                          | v0.6.x      |
| `oodar/`    | Gen 1 C host substrate: 26 capability tokens (14 NORTHSTAR core + 12 future-state), Landlock, OoPathCap, 6-subdir layout, Floor-broken to v3.0.0 (cap-gated oo_read_stdin / oo_file_stamp / oo_metrics_*) | v3.0.0 |
| `oodac/`    | Compiler (extracted from `ooda/`; gemini v0.1.x reorg + cap-ABI alignment with oodar v3.0.0) | v0.1.x |

## 3. Where to go next

- **The submenu.** Each repo has its own page sourced from its
  `openOODA/{repo}/docs/` directory.
- **The 14 capability tokens.** They appear across the LSP, MCP, and
  std visualisations. There is no ambient authority anywhere in the
  stack. (oodar v3.0.0 added the 26th token, `Metrics`, which
  re-uses the v2.1.0-reserved bit 0x800 for the cap-gated
  `oo_metrics_*` family.)
- **The polyrepo topology.** See `openOODA/RULES.oot` §1.23 for the
  why behind the split.

## 5. ooda/ cleanup chain (gated on oodac)

The `ooda/` legacy monorepo cannot be killed until oodac is fully
shipped. The chain (in execution order) is:

1. **oodac ships as a polyrepo sibling** — gemini is working on this
   (`ooda/oodac/` currently at v0.1.4, commit `c9b9e13b8`).
   When gemini signals done, the source in `ooda/oodac/*` is moved
   to the `oodac/` polyrepo and the canonical location flips.
2. **oodac emit backends point at oodar v3.0.0 paths.** Currently
   `ooda/oodac/emit/c/c_emit_temps.oo`, `c_emit_args.oo`,
   `c_emit_preamble.oo`, `ooda/oodac/emit/llvm/llvm_emit_olist.oo`,
   and `ooda/oodac/emit/llvm/llvm_jit_engine.oo` all reference
   `chs_rt_*` paths. After step 1, they flip to
   `oodar/<subdir>/<file>.c` per the v3.0.0 6-subdir layout (and
   thread cap tokens for the v3.0.0 public surface).
3. **Delete `ooda/runtime/`** — 71 stale `chs_rt_*.c` duplicates
   that the oodar extraction (v0.1.0) was meant to kill but
   couldn't because the emit backends still pointed at them.
4. **Delete `ooda/oodac/`** (now empty after step 1).
5. **Audit ooda/ remaining** (`fixtures/`, `qa/`, `scripts/`,
   `tests/`, `site/`, `templates/`, `tools/`, `vendor/`) — anything
   still referenced by the extracted oodac moves with it;
   everything else is dead.
6. **Kill the `ooda/` monorepo** — the final "this is the legacy
   coordination shell, no longer here" commit.

The gate is gemini's "oodac is done" signal. Per the standing rule
("we can't clean up ooda until oodac is done"), nothing in
`ooda/odac/` or `ooda/runtime/` is touched in the meantime.

> The install command lives at the top and bottom of this page —
> it is the same component, not duplicated content.
